[Gravatar](https://gravatar.com) is a global avatar service that links a profile photo to an email address. A user uploads a photo once, and every site or app that looks up the hash of their email address shows the same picture.

# How does the integration work?

Appwrite Avatars looks up Gravatar for you through the [user photos](/docs/products/avatars/user-photos) endpoint. One `getPhoto` call returns the best available profile photo for a user, and Gravatar is one of the sources Appwrite checks. It comes after a photo from the user's OAuth2 identity and before Libravatar, initials, and a built-in placeholder.

Appwrite queries Gravatar with the SHA-256 hash of the user's trimmed, lowercase email address, so you don't need a Gravatar account or API key. When Gravatar has no photo for a hash, Appwrite moves on to the next source, so the call always returns an image.

{% info title="Self-hosted requirements" %}
`getPhoto` is available in self-hosted Appwrite 2.0 and later. Your server needs outbound internet access to reach Gravatar.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To show Gravatar photos with Appwrite Avatars, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Show the signed-in user's photo

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already. Pass `current()` as the `userId` to get the photo of the user who is signed in. If they have a Gravatar for their email address and no higher-priority photo, such as one from an OAuth2 identity, Appwrite returns their Gravatar.

```js
import { Client, Avatars } from "appwrite";

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const avatars = new Avatars(client);

const result = avatars.getPhoto({
    userId: 'current()',
    width: 128,
    height: 128
});

console.log(result); // Resource URL
```

The Web SDK returns a URL that you can use as the `src` of an `<img>` tag. Pass another user's ID instead of `current()` to show their photo, for example next to a comment or in a member list.

## Step 2: Show photos for people who aren't users yet

To show an avatar for someone who doesn't have an account in your project, such as an invitee, pass an `emailHash` and a `name`. The `emailHash` is the SHA-256 hash of the email address, trimmed and lowercased, as a 64-character hex string. Appwrite only accepts the hash, so the address itself never appears in a URL.

```js
async function hashEmail(email) {
    const data = new TextEncoder().encode(email.trim().toLowerCase());
    const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', data);
    return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest))
        .map((byte) => byte.toString(16).padStart(2, '0'))
        .join('');
}

const result = avatars.getPhoto({
    emailHash: await hashEmail('walter@example.com'),
    name: 'Walter White',
    width: 128,
    height: 128
});
```

Appwrite looks the hash up on Gravatar and Libravatar, renders the name as initials if neither has a photo, and falls back to the placeholder.

## Step 3: Limit the image rating

Gravatar rates every image as `g`, `pg`, `r`, or `x`. Use the `rating` parameter to set the highest rating Appwrite accepts. It defaults to `g`, the most restrictive rating.

```js
const result = avatars.getPhoto({
    userId: 'current()',
    rating: 'pg'
});
```

Appwrite sends every photo with `Cache-Control: private, no-store`, so a user's new Gravatar shows up the next time the image loads.

